 currently being brought  under control at its height we had uh  approximately 100 firefighters on scene  uh the situation remains very Dynamic  it's obviously spread out over a wide  area the conditions that our fire crews  are operating in uh are extremely  arduous because of the temperatures that  we're currently facing  um we this was uh one incident amongst  many that London Fire Crews have been  facing throughout today which was the  reason we declared a major incident  earlier uh this afternoon we were  dealing with not only this incident in  Weddington but a substantial incident uh  near upminster and across the capital  we've been dealing with a number of  grassland and Woodland type fires uh  that have been started as a result of  the particular heat wave that we're  facing at the moment and the fact that  the ground is so Tinderbox dry so our  resources today have been under  significant pressure which was part of  the reason for declaring the major  incident earlier today what I should say  though to provide reassurance is that  the London fire brigade is still  responding to emergency calls but we  would ask that when people are requiring  the London Fire brigadies for  life-threatening emergencies and fire  situations  um our resources are being deployed  dynamically across the capital as we  speak to bring these incidents under  control and we would ask actually that  people moderate their behavior over the  course of the next sort of 12 to 24  hours in particular by not having things  like barbecues out in Parkland and  Woodland by making sure that cigarettes  are extinguished properly by making sure  that rubbish and broken glass in  particular is not left lying around  which can cause a spark and Trigger uh  Wildfire  um we have been yeah under significant  pressure today but I'd just like to pay  Testament to our firefighters officers  and control staff who are have worked  extremely hard today to make sure that  the resources of the London fire brigade  have been able to bring these incidents  under control in partnership with our  emergency services in the metropolitan  police and the London Ambulance Service  and assistant commissioner could I ask  you in addition to what you mentioned  there about what the public can do could  I add in perhaps if they spot even a you  know a small Blaze some smoldering going  on in a in a piece of grass it may seem  trivial at that point but would it be  worth reporting on the basis that given  how tender dry given the conditions that  you describe that could easily spread  no that's absolutely right so it's  really really important that people call  999 and ask for the fire brigade when  they do see any fires however small they  may be because as a result of the  temperatures that we've seen over the  last week and in particular the last two  days the ground is extremely dry and  acts like a Tinderbox so even a small  fire will develop very very quickly if  it's not tackled effectively and  efficient efficiently in its early  stages we would also say to people that  they don't try and Tackle fires  themselves the situation that you can  see is extremely Dynamic and these fires  can develop very very quickly and we  would not want to see members of the  public exposing themselves to  unnecessary risk the London fire brigade  and other Fire and Rescue Services  across the UK have very well trained  very well prepared and very well  equipped firefighters to deal with these  incidents so we would urge the public  not to try and take them on themselves  but to make sure that they call us at  the earlier stop opportunity because the  earlier we get that information the  earlier we can mobilize and the earlier  we can bring these incidents under  control  and is it areas talking about greater  London of course it goes for all our  major contributions is it areas like  this on the outskirts of the major  cities where there you know there's a  mix we we all know the terrain there's a  there's a mix of grassland Woodland  maybe some scrub land as well therefore  it's not just all buildings and and  tarmac which I imagine is harder to set  on fire you you're in danger of getting  these situations developing where  something can develop in the Woodland or  in the grass and then spread to  buildings from there  well interestingly London's actually one  of the the sort of greenest cities in in  Europe in terms of the amount of green  space that there is even in even in  central London so you're right to point  to the fact that the suburbs tend to  have more grassland more Fields more  Woodland uh but actually we do see  significant areas of uh green spaces in  central London as well so at first it  may appear that wildfire and grass and  fires in particular are not always a  major issue for urban brigades like  London fire brigade actually that's not  the case we do have significant areas of  green areas in central London and on the  outskirts so it's very much a clear and  present threat for us to make sure that  we're prepared and ready to respond to  these incidents as and when they occur  and just to be very clear for us I just  want to revisit the Declaration of a  major incident assistant commissioner  what does that mean in practice what are  the ramifications of it of course it  tells the public how serious the  situation is you've already said that  there is uh no then knock on danger to  the existing cover and safety that the  fire service provide for us but what  does it mean in terms of resources for  you do you bring people back from a  day's leave do you have access to other  forces other parts of the emergency  services to help out  well what it really does is it triggers  a lot of our multi-agency Arrangements  that we've got in place for London so  when any one agency or more than one  agency declares a major incident what it  does is it triggers well-rehearsed and  well practiced planning Arrangements  across uh not only the Emergency  Services uh but local government and and  the mayor's office uh as well as partner  agencies to make sure that actually what  we've got is the full force of London's  um services and partner agencies working  together in a collaborative way to bring  this incident under control because  although what you're seeing primarily at  the moment are fire related issues they  of course have a knock-on impact on  people's housing on people's welfare on  people's health and safety and so what  we need to do is make sure that we've  got that coordinated pan- London  approach bringing together all of the  key agencies working to a cohesive  strategy uh to work to bring not only  the fires and the other emergencies  under control all but to get London back  to normal as soon as possible so by  declaring a major incident what that  does is it triggers a lot of that key  partnership working which then enables  us to get back to a sense of normality  as soon as possible and operationally  specifically looking at Wellington what  is the information the latest you have  on how much under control that is now by  the fire service  so the situation is being brought under  control it Still Remains very very  dynamic because  um as you can see in in your pictures  where you've got areas that appear to be  out they can reignite very very quickly  particularly given the very very dry  nature of the land that it's currently  on so we are going to have our resources  there for a significant period of time  making sure that we're turning over and  damping down to prevent any further  escalation of the incident and stopping  any chance of a reignition which is  something that is a real prevalence when  you're dealing with these types of  incidents because of the nature of the  ground and its dried condition at the  moment any small spark or any area that  we do not put out properly could run the  risk of reignition so we will have  resources there throughout the night  making sure that we're bringing this  into them under control  and what you've been describing to a  senator system commissioner is I suppose  resilience in action this is an incident  which clearly you're not aware it's  going to happen but given the  circumstances given the forecast you  were looking at given the the training  and the coordination you've described to  us between the variety of emergency  services this is something that you've  been well prepared for and been able to  respond to very quickly  that's right so we've got well rehearsed  multi-agency training in place to make  sure uh that we're able to respond to  these types of incidents as and when  they occur we've obviously had  experience recently as last year around  wide area flooding for example which had  a similar pan London impact uh and  required the full resource of the London  fire brigade to be able to bring those  incidents under control  these climate-related challenges that we  know we are now facing are things that  we are for properly putting into our  longer term strategic planning to make  sure that the Brigade is as resourced  and as prepared as they possibly can be  to deal with these incidents as and when  they occur but the the position that  London finds itself in is that we have  got well rehearsed well-practiced  multi-agency plans in place to deal with  exactly this type of challenge when it's  actually pan London  um and actually that's been brought to  bear throughout today and as I say I  would I would just once again  put credit towards our firefighters and  officers and control staff and emergency  service Partners who have worked  incredibly hard and extremely arduous  conditions to bring these incidents  under control and Mr Swift what is the  latest on the people involved in this  incident in Wellington we could see at  least six rather large houses in a row  well virtually destroyed certainly the  roof's gone and been hosed down by the  fire service are there any reports of  any casualties or has everyone either  been outdoor or got out safely  so we've not had formal reports on the  number of casualties come through yet  because as you can see this Still  Remains a very Dynamic uh incident so  we're still waiting on the latest  information as far as casualty numbers  are concerned what I can say is that  we've got significant resource at the  scene and bringing this incident under  control as quickly as possible but  obviously this will be subject uh to a  significant investigation to try and  ascertain the cause of the fire but at  the moment uh I'm unable to comment on  any potential casualties well thank you  for that it's going to be a long night  though isn't it it's going to be a long  week it's going to be a a long summer  how you you talk about the preparedness  but the resources you have to deploy how  long can they keep it up for  well as I say we've got well rehearsed  plans in place we've got relief  Arrangements in place so our crews who  will be coming on to the night shift uh  in a couple of hours time will be  getting ready and prepared so that they  can take over from Crews on on who have  been operating throughout today and that  will continue uh throughout the week  we've got proper plans in place we  understand that this is a long-term  challenge that we need to we need to  face but because of those plans because  of the training that we've got and  because of our ability to coordinate our  response across the capital we're  confident that we're able to to bring  these incidents under control and then  to make sure we've got the resources  available for what will continue to be a  very challenging week for us  assistant commissioner Jonathan swifter  very reassuring talking to you very  informative of course thank you very  much indeed we know you've got an awful  lot to deal with uh thank you very much  indeed for sparing the time to talk to  us on Sky News
 

